      "text": "To determine how many positive factors of 96 are also multiples of 12, we start by finding the prime factorization of 96 and 12. The prime factorization of 96 is:\n\n\\[ 96 = 2^5 \\times 3 \\]\n\nThe prime factorization of 12 is:\n\n\\[ 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\]\n\nA factor of 96 that is also a multiple of 12 must be of the form \\(2^a \\times 3^b\\) where \\(2 \\leq a \\leq 5\\) and \\(1 \\leq b \\leq 1\\). This is because the factor must include at least \\(2^2\\) (since 12 has \\(2^2\\)) and 3 raised to the power of 1 (since 12 has 3).\n\nSo, we need to count the possible values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n- \\(a\\) can be 2, 3, 4, or 5 (4 choices).\n- \\(b\\) can be 1 (1 choice).\n\nTherefore, the total number of factors of 96 that are multiples of 12 is:\n\n\\[ 4 \\times 1 = 4 \\]\n\nThese factors are: \\(2^2 \\times 3^1 = 12\\), \\(2^3 \\times 3^1 = 24\\), \\(2^4 \\times 3^1 = 48\\), and \\(2^5 \\times 3^1 = 96\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
